export const CLIENT_226_ARCHITECTURE = [
  [
    "Completed output",
    "The common architecture is a sequence of gates: <b>admit, process, stabilize, convert, retain</b>. In cartilage, recruitment feeds matrix production, organized expansion and bone conversion. In cognition, initiation feeds encoding, relational binding, consolidation and later retrieval. A fuller intermediate store is useful only while its contents remain viable and can reach the next stage."
  ],
  [
    "Capacity before amplification",
    "Each gate has a processing capacity and a finite buffer. Faster translation can congest folding and export; stronger excitation can congest discrimination and consolidation. When arrivals outrun clearance, the queue becomes a source of stress. The highest-leverage intervention then increases downstream capacity, reduces loss, or meters entry; adding more upstream drive enlarges the blockage."
  ],
  [
    "Geometry carries function",
    "The same material behaves differently when its connections change. A collagen network must contain swelling without locking the expanding cell in place; a neural network must retain a representation without allowing it to recruit every competing representation. <b>Local compliance within global constraint</b> connects matrix anisotropy with selective circuit inhibition. Stiffness and inhibition are useful in the right location, harmful when spread indiscriminately."
  ],
  [
    "State carries history",
    "Administration, tissue exposure, receptor response and structural persistence run on different clocks. Clearing a compound does not undo a cross-link, replace a receptor, or erase a synaptic tag. A phase transition therefore depends on the state left behind: cargo cleared, scaffold ready, inhibitory gradient restored, or learning corrected. Calendar windows locate the intended transition; residual activity determines whether it has occurred."
  ],
  [
    "Shared resources join distant targets",
    "ATP, oxygen, calcium handling, methyl donors, membrane turnover, perfusion and sleep connect pathways that look separate on a receptor map. Two modest demands can exhaust the same reserve together. Conversely, one capacity repair can support several outputs. The compact base belongs at these shared constraints; additional compounds belong at a named, remaining bottleneck."
  ],
  [
    "Preservation and release",
    "Protection changes sign when the protected intermediate must be processed. Matrix preservation can obstruct terminal cartilage removal; stable working-memory activity can obstruct flexible updating; translational restraint can prevent overload before later limiting consolidation. The organizing principle is <b>protect what is being built, release what must move, retain what has been completed</b>."
  ]
];

